# Template only — never put a real key in this file, and never create a
# real `.env` alongside it. This file is committed; `.env` is gitignored.
#
# ── How this app actually resolves credentials ────────────────────────
# app/ai_providers/config.py's get_api_key() checks, in order:
#   1. macOS Keychain  (service "creative-asset-engine", account
#      "<provider>_api_key")  ← the intended mechanism for local dev
#   2. the <PROVIDER>_API_KEY environment variable  ← for CI / controlled
#      deployment environments, where a Keychain isn't available
#
# NOTE: nothing in this codebase calls load_dotenv(). A `.env` file is
# therefore inert on its own — the variables below only take effect if
# your shell or process supervisor actually exports them. Historically a
# real `.env` sat here containing a live key that the app never read; it
# was removed during the Phase 0 remediation pass. Prefer the Keychain
# locally, and real environment variables (not a file) in CI.
#
# ── Provider → account mapping ────────────────────────────────────────
# Google's Gemini analysis models and the Nano Banana image-generation
# models share ONE credential by deliberate design: gemini_adapter.py
# calls get_api_key("nano_banana"), not get_api_key("gemini"). So there
# is no GEMINI_API_KEY below — rotating the Nano Banana credential
# rotates Gemini too.

# OpenAI — OCR, product isolation, vision analysis, text/prompt
# generation, and the gpt-image-1 image-generation fallback.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Google (Gemini analysis + Nano Banana image generation, one key).
NANO_BANANA_API_KEY=

# ── Setting the Keychain entries instead (recommended locally) ────────
# python3 -c "import keyring; keyring.set_password('creative-asset-engine', 'openai_api_key', '...')"
# python3 -c "import keyring; keyring.set_password('creative-asset-engine', 'nano_banana_api_key', '...')"
